{
  "ID": "3.3",
  "Context/Background": "To find novel treatments and drug combinations that could effectively target colorectal cancer cells (CRCs) and lower the probability of disease relapse, we designed multiple different combinations between cisplatin, cannabidiol (CBD), and intermittent serum starvation (ISS) on colorectal cancer cell lines. We analyzed differentially expressed genes and affected pathways in colorectal cancer cell lines to understand further the potential molecular mechanisms behind the treatments and their interactions.",
  "Prompt": "Based on the results of the differential expression analysis of ISS vs DMSO in colorectal cancer cells, run a pathway overrepresentation analysis for statistically significant genes (up and down combined) against the Reactome database. Then, provide a discussion of any relevant affected pathways, highlighting important up and down regulated genes in each.  Focus on any mechanisms by which intermittent serum starvation may alter cancer related pathways.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-12 13:20:06",
  "elapsed_seconds": 511.25,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Pathway Over‐Representation Analysis (Reactome)  \n------------------------------------------------  \nWe first filtered the ISS vs. DMSO DEG table for adjusted p-value < 0.05 (n ≈ 6 555 genes, both up- and down-regulated) and submitted the resulting gene list to Reactome ORA (ora_pipe, Reactome_Pathways_2024).  Key enriched pathways (adjusted p < 1 × 10⁻⁵) included:  \n\n• Membrane Trafficking (Overlap = 302/633, padj ≈ 4.3 × 10⁻¹⁵)  \n  Genes of note: SEC23A, KDELR1–3, ARF1/3/5, COPA/B1/B2, RAB1A, USO1, GOSR1/2.  \n\n• Signaling by Rho GTPases, Miro GTPases and RHOBTB3 (Overlap = 308/688, padj ≈ 4.6 × 10⁻¹¹)  \n  Genes of note: RHOA, RAC1/2/3, CDC42, RHOB, RHOBTB3, ARHGEF1/2/3, RHOC, VAV1.  \n\n• Intra-Golgi and Retrograde Golgi-to-ER Traffic (Overlap = 109/204, padj ≈ 9.9 × 10⁻⁹)  \n  Genes of note: COG1–7, TRAPPC3/6A/6B/9/10, COPA/B, KDELRs, SEC24A/C/D.  \n\n• Regulation of Cholesterol Biosynthesis by SREBP (Overlap = 42/56, padj ≈ 6.7 × 10⁻⁹)  \n  Genes of note: SREBF1/2, HMGCR, HMGCS1, MVK, FDFT1, FDPS, LSS, SQLE, SCAP, MBTPS1/2.  \n\n• TP53-Mediated Transcriptional Programs  \n  – “TP53 Regulates Transcription of Cell Cycle Genes” (Overlap = 33/49, padj ≈ 2.1 × 10⁻⁵)  \n    Genes: TP53, PLK2/3, CDC25C, CCNB1, BAX, SFN, BTG2, E2F4.  \n  – “TP53 Regulates Transcription of Additional Cell Cycle Genes…” (Overlap = 17/21, padj ≈ 1.9 × 10⁻⁴)  \n    Genes: TP53, CNOT family members, PLAGL1, RGCC.  \n\n• Unfolded Protein Response (UPR)/ER Stress  \n  – “IRE1α Activates Chaperones” (Overlap = 33/51, padj ≈ 5.8 × 10⁻⁵)  \n    Genes: ERN1, XBP1, HSPA5, DNAJB9/11, PDIA5/6.  \n  – “ATF6 Activates Chaperones” (Overlap = 10/12, padj ≈ 6.5 × 10⁻³)  \n    Genes: ATF6, DDIT3, HSP90B1, CALR, MBTPS1/2.  \n\nDiscussion  \n----------  \n1. Intermittent serum starvation (ISS) in CRC cells triggers a coordinated repression of lipid‐biosynthetic programs.  Reactome ORA shows the SREBP‐driven cholesterol-biosynthesis module (SREBF1/2, HMGCR, FDFT1, SQLE, etc.) as one of the most highly over‐represented pathways (padj ≈ 6.7 × 10⁻⁹), consistent with nutrient deprivation down-regulating membrane lipid synthesis.  \n\n2. Concomitantly, ISS engages ER stress and the UPR: both IRE1α- and ATF6-mediated chaperone activation pathways are significantly enriched (padj ≈ 5.8 × 10⁻⁵ and 6.5 × 10⁻³, respectively), with up-regulation of HSPA5 (BiP), DDIT3 (CHOP), XBP1 and ERN1.  This suggests that serum withdrawal imposes proteostatic stress in CRCs.  \n\n3. Cell‐cycle checkpoints are reinforced via p53 transcriptional programs.  Multiple TP53 target gene sets (including PLK2/3, CDC25C, BAX, BTG2, SFN) are over‐represented (padj ~ 10⁻⁵–10⁻⁴), indicating ISS may induce a DNA‐damage or nutrient‐stress checkpoint leading to G₂ arrest or apoptosis.  \n\n4. Membrane trafficking and Golgi-ER retrograde transport modules (e.g., COPI/COPII coat components, ARF GTPases, COG/TRAPPC complexes) are among the most significant terms (padj ~ 10⁻⁸–10⁻¹⁵).  Altered vesicular transport likely reflects adaptive remodeling of the secretory pathway under nutrient limitation.  \n\n5. Cytoskeletal and Rho GTPase signaling pathways are enriched (padj ~ 10⁻¹¹), implicating changes in cell‐shape, motility and migration machinery (RHOA, CDC42, RACs), which may contribute to altered invasiveness upon ISS.  \n\nMechanistic Implications  \n-----------------------  \nBy simultaneously down-tuning lipid anabolism and up-regulating stress‐response and cell‐cycle checkpoint networks, ISS appears to starve CRC cells of key building blocks while enforcing proteostatic and genotoxic barriers to proliferation.  Perturbation of Golgi-ER trafficking and Rho GTPase signaling further suggests major reprogramming of membrane and cytoskeletal homeostasis, all of which could sensitize CRCs to chemotherapeutics or immune‐based clearance.",
